 # Ruby Method Argument Conventions

- **Always use keyword arguments:** When defining or calling methods, prefer keyword arguments over positional arguments. This improves clarity by explicitly naming each argument.
  ```ruby
  # Good
def process_data(user:, settings:, timeout: 30)
  # ...
end

process_data(user: current_user, settings: app_settings)

# Less clear
def process_data(user, settings, timeout = 30)
  # ...
end

process_data(current_user, app_settings)
  ```

- **Use shorthand syntax for matching keyword arguments:** When the variable or method you are passing as a keyword argument has the same name as the keyword argument itself, use Ruby's shorthand syntax (available in Ruby 2.7+).
  ```ruby
  user = find_user
  settings = load_settings

  # Good (using shorthand)
process_data(user:, settings:)

  # Avoid (redundant)
process_data(user: user, settings: settings)
  ```



